 ## Overview
-Entry Point Adjustment is a hardware-accelerated volume rendering technique that enables the camera to move inside the volume's bounding box without the view disappearing or clipping artifacts. It solves the problem of standard ray casting failing when the camera origin exceeds the back face of the volume by dynamically shifting the ray's entry point forward along the ray direction based on a scale coefficient [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard direct volume ray casting where the ray always starts at the camera position, this method decouples the visual entry point from the physical camera position to maintain continuous visibility [S1, unofficial].
+Entry Point Adjustment is a hardware-accelerated technique for direct volume ray casting that enables the camera to move inside the volume's bounding box without the view disappearing or clipping artifacts. It solves the problem of ray origin placement when the viewer penetrates the front face of the volume by dynamically shifting the ray entry point forward along the ray direction based on a scale coefficient, allowing the user to "flow through" the volume [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray casting which assumes the camera is always outside the front face, this method maintains visual continuity by adjusting the start of the ray march to remain within the volume bounds defined by the exit point [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in a two-pass pipeline. First, the exit points of rays passing through the volume are pre-computed. Second, the front face is rendered, and for each pixel, the pre-computed exit point is used to define the ray segment, which is then marched through the volume texture.
+The method operates in a two-pass rendering pipeline using framebuffer objects (FBOs) and fragment shaders. It relies on the geometric relationship between the front and back faces of the volume's bounding box.
 
